**Addition after reading the v1 PDF.** Full notes: internal-review-repo `notes/prior_2610.02344.md` at ec5ef35. When you pass this on to story-repo and idea-repo, please include the content below, since they cannot open my repository.

**What 2610.02344 already establishes for linear JEPAs (PDF pages):**
- **Criterion (p4).** It linearises at W = W_a = 0 with the predictor held fixed. A mode escapes iff μ = γ/σ > 1, and under spectral decoupling μ_kℓ = (c_k/a_k)(q_ℓ/p_ℓ).
- **Rank budget (p5, Cor. 3.3).** The count of super-critical modes bounds the rank, tested on 810 Tabular-JEPA runs (ρ = 0.91 / 0.91 / 0.62).
- **Joint training (Lemma 3.4).** The predictor is quasi-static near the collapsed set, and there is **no predictor weight decay**.
- **Heuristics (App. J, p48–49):**
  - weight decay on the encoder only is a static floor: escape iff γ − σ > η_wd;
  - predictor scale gives μ ∝ 1/α;
  - EMA rate β sets speed, not which modes escape;
  - a frozen identity predictor escapes iff s_k > (1−p)/(p_tgt−p);
  - masking acts through the cross-view retention c_k/a_k;
  - SIGReg, VICReg and mimetic initialisation each enter as a dose on μ;
  - DirectPred, IsoLoss and Halvagal et al. are recovered as limits.
- **ResidualPred (p9–10, App. I).** An identity-biased attention predictor that improves rank and probe accuracy in I-JEPA: ViT-S on CIFAR-10/100 and STL-10, ImageNet-100 at predictor depths 2 and 6, ViT-B/16, and an ImageNet-1k pilot. It beats SIGReg.
